# Infrared Solution-Processed Quantum Dot Solar Cells Reaching External Quantum Efficiency of 80% at 1.35 µm and Jsc in Excess of 34 mA cm-2

## abstract
Developing low-cost photovoltaic absorbers that can harvest the short-wave infrared (SWIR) part of the solar spectrum, which remains unharnessed by current Si-based and perovskite photovoltaic technologies, is a prerequisite for making high-efficiency, low-cost tandem solar cells. Here, infrared PbS colloidal quantum dot (CQD) solar cells employing a hybrid inorganic-organic ligand exchange process that results in an external quantum efficiency of 80% at 1.35 µm are reported, leading to a short-circuit current density of 34 mA cm-2 and a power conversion efficiency (PCE) up to 7.9%, which is a current record for SWIR CQD solar cells. When this cell is placed at the back of an MAPbI3 perovskite film, it delivers an extra 3.3% PCE by harnessing light beyond 750 nm.

Infrared Solution-Processed Quantum Dot Solar Cells Reaching External Quantum Efficiency of $80\%$ at 1.35 µm and J in Excess of 34 mA cm−2
Developing low-cost photovoltaic absorbers that can harvest the short-wave infrared (SWIR) part of the solar spectrum, which remains unharnessed by current Si-based and perovskite photovoltaic technologies, is a prerequisite for making high-efficiency, low-cost tandem solar cells. Here, infrared PbS colloidal quantum dot (CQD) solar cells employing a hybrid inorganic–organic ligand exchange process that results in an external quantum efficiency of $80\%$ at $\mathbf{1.35\upmum}$ are reported, leading to a short-circuit current density of $34m\mathsf{A}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ and a power conversion efficiency (PCE) up to $7.9\%$ , which is a current record for SWIR CQD solar cells. When this cell is placed at the back of an $\mathsf{M A P b l}_{3}$ perovskite film, it delivers an extra $3.3\%$ PCE by harnessing light beyond $750\mathsf{n m}$ .
Colloidal quantum dots (CQDs) have been extensively studied as a third-generation solar cell technology.[1–5] PbS CQD with a wide bandgap tuning range could be used for harvesting the near and short-wave infrared (SWIR) part of the solar spectrum, at which silicon and perovskite solar cells are transparent. Recently, efforts have been focused on perovskite or Si-based tandem solar cells, as means to surpass Si efficiency records and reduce the per Watt cost of PV systems.[6–9] A theoretical power conversion efficiency (PCE) up to $42\%$ is predicted in a tandem solar cell comprising two current matched subcells with bandgaps of 1.6 and $0.95\mathrm{~eV},$ respectively, which is substantially higher than the $31\%$ single junction limit.[10,11] While an abundance of materials can potentially serve as the $1.6~\mathrm{eV}$ absorber in tandem solar cells, the same is not true for the $0.95\ \mathrm{eV}$ one. Hence, here we focus on developing the lower bandgap subcell. PbS CQD with tunable bandgap $(0.7{-}2.1\ \mathrm{eV})$ makes an excellent choice in view of their low cost, solution processability, and bandgap tunability,[12] especially $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{S}$ QD with a first exciton peak around $1.3{\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{{m}}}}\ (0.95\ \mathrm{eV})$ , which have been proposed to complement ideally with lead-halide perovskites in tandem solar cells.[13] The efficiencies of PbS QD single junction solar cells have improved substantially up to $11.6\%$ , thanks to advances in device structure optimization and surface passivation strategies.[14,15] These have been achieved with CQDs whose bandgap is around 1.3–1.4 eV. However, little attention has been paid to lower bandgap $(<1$ eV) PbS solar cells. In the past, a $7.3\%$ efficiency has been reported from 1 eV bandgap PbS QD solar cells[13] Air-stable $1.3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ PbS solar cells based on a novel one-step fabrication process have also been reported, yet their PCE was 3.5%.[16]
Overall, in order to achieve highly efficient and stable PbS QD solar cells beyond $1100\ \mathrm{nm}$ , several issues need to be tackled. For example, (i) new strategies should be applied for the surface passivation on large PbS QDs, which serve not only to minimize the surface trap density but also to favorably tune the band levels of the QD film to facilitate efficient electron injection in the electron acceptor layer.[17,18] (ii) Band alignment engineering should be reconsidered for the purpose of optimizing the contacts of the PbS QD active layer. The energy levels of QDs shift with their size.[19] Thus for the $1.3\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ PbS QD active layer, an electron-accepting layer with deeper conduction band level may be needed instead of the usual $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers.[16,20] (iii) The stability of the large PbS QDs solar cells needs to be studied and improved. PbS QD solar cells with different size QDs have been reported with a large variation in their stability.[17,21] Specifically, the air stability of the cells has shown an abrupt transition to the worse when crossing a diameter of $4\mathrm{nm}$ in PbS QDs.[21] Having these issues in mind, here we report a low-temperature synthesis of stable $1.3\mathrm{-}1.4~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ PbS QDs by using a multiinjection method. A hybrid inorganic–organic ligand treatment combining zinc iodide $(\mathrm{ZnI}_{2})$ and 3-mercaptapropionic acid (MPA) has been used to surface passivate the as-obtained PbS QDs. In addition, a thin layer of aluminum-doped $z\mathrm{nO}$ (AZO) deposited via sputtering was introduced as the electron-accepting layer of the solar cell. As a result, an external quantum efficiency (EQE) up to $80\%$ at the first exciton peak around $1.35~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ has been achieved in these devices, resulting in a short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ of $34\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and a PCE of $7.9\%$ .
For the synthesis of $1.35~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ bandgap PbS QDs, instead of using one of the recipes available in the literature,[22–26] we have followed a low-temperature synthetic approach by using a multiinjection method with minor modifications[26] (the detailed synthesis procedures can be found in the Experimental Section). Absorption spectra, transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image, and the size distribution histogram of the as-obtained PbS QDs are shown in the Figure S1 (Supporting Information). In Figure 1a, a cross-sectional focused ion beam scanning electron microscopy (FIB-SEM) image of the PbS QD solar cell is presented. $30~\mathrm{nm}$ of AZO sputtered on top of cleaned indium tin oxide (ITO) substrate was used as the electron-accepting layer, followed by the layer-by-layer deposition method for PbS QDs film. An additional thin 1,2-ethanedithiol (EDT)- treated PbS QD film was deposited atop as the electron-blocking layer with a thickness of $40\mathrm{nm}$ . In the end, the device was completed by depositing $80\ \mathrm{nm}$ of Au as the back electrode (the detailed device fabrication process can be found in the Experimental Section). The most critical determinant factor for high performance is the selection of an appropriate passivation scheme. It serves to minimize recombination, increase carrier transport, and tune the band levels of the PbS QD film favorably for charge extraction. In identifying the optimal ligand chemistry, organic–inorganic mixed ligand strategy using $\mathrm{\ZnI_{2-}M P A}$ . We hypothesized that the mixed ligand strategy would serve better since it would not downshift the energy levels of large $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{S}$ QDs to the extent that pure halide passivation has been reported to do.[12] The device performance of the EMII and $\mathrm{ZnI}_{2-}\mathrm{MPA}-$ treated solar cells is summarized in Table 1. The $\mathrm{ZnI}_{2-}\mathrm{MPA}-$ treated device significantly outperforms the EMII-treated one (PCE of $6.7\%$ and $1.1\%$ , respectively). The EMII-treated device suffers from inefficient charge extraction as evidenced by the extremely low $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ $(11.77~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ . To account for this, we sought the origin in the band levels of these two PbS QD films employing UV photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS). The comparison of the UPS data between EMII- and $\mathrm{\ZnI_{2-}M P A}$ -treated PbS QD films is shown in Figure S2 (Supporting Information). $\mathrm{ZnI}_{2}.$ _MPA-treated PbS QD films possess shallower conduction band compared to the EMII counterpart, thus forming a favorable electron-accepting junction with AZO. Hence, with an additional EDT-treated PbS layer (as shown in Figure 1b) as the efficient electron-blocking layer, the $\mathrm{ZnI}_{2}$ _MPA-treated PbS QDs film results in an efficient QD solar cell. AZO was selected instead of $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ in order to facilitate a more favorable we focused on two strategies that we reported previously in near-infrared (NIR) PbS QD solar cells with exciton peak at $950~\mathrm{nm}$ .[4,27] One is based on a pure halide passivation employing 1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium iodide (EMII) as the halide precursor ligand, whereas the second was based on a hybrid electron-accepting band offset when in contact with the SWIR PbS QD film (Figure 1b). Devices based on $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanocrystals have also been fabricated and tested as shown in Table S1 (Supporting Information), and they are outperformed by the ones based on AZO.
Device performance of the SWIR PbS QD solar cells was further optimized by performing a thickness-dependent study. A thickness of $420~\mathrm{nm}$ for the active layer has led to the best performing devices (Table S2, Supporting Information). Current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of the optimized device with forward and reversed scans are shown in Figure 1c. The highest $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $34\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ was measured, leading to a PCE of $7.9\%$ . In addition, negligible $J{-}V$ hysteresis loss was observed in this device. The inset plot in Figure 1c shows the simulated $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ as a function of the active layer thickness $(100-600\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) by using the transfer matrix method (TMM),[28–30] while keeping other layer thicknesses intact. The optical modeling yields a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $35\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for a thickness of $420{\-}440\ \mathrm{nm}$ in very good agreement with the experimental data. Figure 1d illustrates the experimentally measured and optically simulated EQE spectra of the best performing $420~\mathrm{nm}$ thick device, being in very good agreement. The origin of the record $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ stems from an average EQE of $90\%$ in the visible region of $400{\mathrm{-}}700~\mathrm{nm}$ and a remarkably high EQE $(\approx80\%)$ in the first exciton peak region $(1.3\mathrm{-}1.4~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ . This demonstrates a broad efficient spectral response with an integrated $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of 33.8 and $34.6\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, in measured and simulated EQE, which is consistent with the high $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ observed in the $J{-}V$ measurements under AM1.5 condition. It is worth noting that TMM-simulated $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and EQE values solely rely on optical modeling, which ignores carrier transport and collection losses. The agreement of the optical simulations with the experimental data indicates that the $\mathrm{ZnI}_{2}$ _MPA-treated $1.35~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ PbS QD films are well passivated, and thus lead to a near unity charge collection efficiency despite having a $420\mathrm{nm}$ thick QD absorber.
In Figure 2c we plot the recombination rate $(R)$ , defined as the ratio of the photogenerated charge carriers $(n)$ over $\tau_{\mathfrak{r}}$ , as a function of carrier density n. R shows a near second-order dependence ( $R\propto n^{p}$ , $p\approx1.9$ ), suggestive of free carrier recombination as the dominant recombination process with some limited involvement of traps in the recombination dynamics. (A value close to unity points to free-to-bound charge recombination through traps and a value greater than two indicates Auger recombination.[35]) Intensity-dependent $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and photocurrent $(J_{\mathrm{ph}})$ measurements give additional insights into the charge generation and recombination mechanisms in the device. Figure 2d shows the variation of $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and $J_{\mathrm{ph}}$ with the intensity of incident light. The $J_{\mathrm{ph}}$ shows a near unity intensity dependence $(J_{\mathrm{ph}}\propto\Phi^{m}$ , where $\Phi$ is intensity, $m=0.98$ ) indicative of generation-limited photocurrent. $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ intensity dependence follows the relation, $V_{\mathrm{OC}}\approx\frac{\eta k T}{q}\ln(\Phi),$ where $k T$ is the thermal energy, $\boldsymbol{q}$ is the elementary charge, and $\eta$ is the diode ideality factor. The value of $\eta$ obtained is 1.41, which points to the presence of trap-assisted recombination, yet it is still better than most of the halide-treated NIR PbS-based devices, reported between 1.5 and 1.8.[33,36,37] Based on these findings, we conclude that with the hybrid $\mathrm{\ZnI_{2-}M P A}$ ligand treatment we preserve, to a large extent, the solar grade quality in large $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{S}$ QDs with exciton peak at $1350\mathrm{nm}$ . It allows us to develop high-efficiency infrared solar cells.
To assess the potential of this device to act as a subcell in combination with other established PV technologies that can harness more efficiently the visible and NIR parts of the solar spectrum, we have measured the performance of the SWIR PbS QD solar cell by using a methylammonium lead iodide $(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3})$ perovskite filter (long pass $750~\mathrm{nm}$ or a silicon filter $(1100\ \mathrm{nm}$ long pass) between the device and the AM1.5 solar simulator. The device performance of the SWIR cells with and without optical filtering is summarized in Table 2. The PCE is $3.33\%$ when using an $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite thin film of $350~\mathrm{nm}$ in thickness and a bandgap of $1.6\ \mathrm{eV}.$ Figure 3 illustrates the $J{-}V$ characteristics and the corresponding EQE spectra of the SWIR PbS QD solar cell in the presence and absence of the perovskite thin film in front of the cell. Even when a silicon wafer is placed in front of the cell, the PCE is $0.67\%$ thanks to the extended absorption range in the SWIR that can be harnessed up to $1400\mathrm{nm}$ .
Besides high $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ and PCE, device stability is another key parameter for solar cells.[38,39] In the past, the stability of such devices was found to be poor when only MPA was used for the ligand exchange treatment.[40,41] In our case, to perform preliminary photostability studies, we exposed nonencapsulated devices under ambient air conditions, continuously under AM1.5 solar illumination while performing $J{-}V$ measurements. The evolution of the device performance as a function of time is shown in the Figure 4a. Device performance improves slightly at the beginning likely due to the light soaking of AZO layer, stabilizing later at the maximum value, and remaining unchanged over $^\textrm{\scriptsize5h}$ of continuous AM1.5 illumination. To further demonstrate the stability of the devices, their performance was monitored during $90\mathrm{~d~}$ as a long-term stability test as shown in Figure 4b. $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ improves with time, while $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF remain constant for the first $30\mathrm{~d~}$ , leading to slightly improved PCE from $7.5\%$ to $7.9\%$ . Subsequently, $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ further increases up to $36\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , yet $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF decrease, resulting in a PCE of $7.5\%$ after $90\mathrm{~d~}$ . Overall the solar cell performance remains the same after $90\mathrm{d}$ of storage.
## Experimental Section
PbS Quantum Dot Synthesis: $1.35\upmu\mathrm{m}$ PbS QDs are synthesized via a reported multiinjection method with minor modification. Typically, PbO $(0.45~\mathrm{g})$ was dissolved in a mixture of octadecene (ODE) $(50~\mathsf{m L})$ and oleic acid $(3.8~\mathsf{m L})$ at $95~^{\circ}C$ under vacuum for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . The temperature was adjusted to $80~^{\circ}C$ . Different amounts of hexamethyldisilathiane (TMS) ( $60~\upmu\upiota$ for the first injection and $25~\upmu\upiota$ for the additional three injections) were dissolved in ODE $(3m L)$ . The solution of $60~\upmu\up L$ TMS was injected into the lead precursor solution at $80^{\circ}C$ , and the additional three injections were sequentially followed every fixed time. When the injection finished, the flask was allowed to gradually cool down to room temperature under stirring. QDs were purified in air by adding acetone, followed by centrifugation. The final QDs were dispersed in toluene with a concentration of $40\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ for the solar cell fabrication.
Fabrication of Solar Cells: ITO substrates were cleaned thoroughly in the soap water, water, acetone, and isopropanol respectively in ultrasonic bath. Al-doped ZnO was deposited on the top of the clean ITO substrates by an RC magnetron sputtering (AJA Orion 8 HV) at the room temperature. The $30\ \mathsf{n m}\ \mathsf{A Z O}$ was sputtered in an $\mathsf{A r/}$ $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ mixture (flux ratio of 18:2) at a pressure of 1.4 mTorr at a rate of $0.3\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\mathsf{S}^{-1}$ . PbS layer was deposited by a layer-by-layer spin coating method. PbS QDs with concentration of $40\mathrm{~g~L^{-1}}$ covered the whole substrate, followed by spinning at 2500 rpm for 20 s. $Z n|_{2}/{\mathsf{M P A}}$ $(25\times10^{-3}$ m with $0.01\%$ MPA in methanol) solution covered the PbS layer for 5 s before spinning at 2500 rpm for $\rceil0\mathrm{~s~}$ . The spinning substrate was flushed twice by few drops of methanol and spun for $20~\mathsf{s}$ to make film dry. The above process was repeated till desired thickness achieved. The final two layers of $\mathsf{P b S}$ were treated with $0.02\%$ v/v EDT. The $\mathsf{P b S}$ film was covered by EDT acetonitrile solution for $30~\mathsf{s}$ before spun at 2500 rpm for $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{10~s}}$ The spinning substrate was flushed by 10 drops of acetonitrile followed by spinning for $20~\mathsf{s}$ to make film dry. The above process was repeated twice to obtain EDT-treated PbS QD layer. All the active layer fabrication process was carried out in a fume hood under ambient conditions. $80\ \mathsf{n m}$ Au was deposited on the films by thermal evaporation at a speed of $\rceil\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ by using a Kurt J. Lesker Nano 36 system at a base pressure lower than $10^{-6}$ mbar. The solar cells were transferred from the evaporator and transferred into the glove box for annealing at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $5\min$ . The active area of the device is $0.0374c m^{2}$ . All the devices were taken out of the glovebox and stored in the air for the further characterizations.
$J-V$ Characterizations: All the devices were characterized in the air under ambient conditions. The current density–voltage measurements were carried out using a Keithley 2400 source under AM1.5 illuminations (Oriel sol 3A, Newport Corporation). The accuracy of the measurements was determined as $1\%$ . For the photostability test, the $J-V$ measurements were performed from time to time on the solar cells, which were continuously exposed to the AM1.5 illumination in the air.
EQE Measurements: EQE spectra were recorded with a lock-in amplifier (Stanford Research System SR830) under chopped monochromatic light generated by white light source from a xenon lamp passing through a Newport Cornerstone 260 monochromator. The output power was corrected with Newport 818-UV and Newport 838-IR photodetectors.
UPS Characterization: UPS measurements were performed with a SPECS PHOIBOS 150 hemispherical analyzer (SPECS GmbH, Berlin, Germany) under ultrahigh vacuum conditions (10–10 mbar). UPS measurements were performed with a monochromatic HeI UV source (21.2 eV).
C–V Measurement: The capacitance–voltage (C–V) measurement was performed with Agilent B1500A semiconductor analyzer measurement unit. The AC bias voltage amplitude was set at $50~\mathrm{mV}$ with frequency $1~\mathsf{k H z}$ . The acquired $C{-}V$ data were processed with Mott–Schottky analysis for determining built-in potential and charge density and depletion width.
Intensity-Dependent $J_{\mathsf{p h}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ Measurement: The intensitydependent $J_{\mathsf{p h}}$ and $V_{\mathsf{O C}}$ was performed with Keithley 2400 source meter. The Illumination intensity of AM1.5 was maintained using a class AAA solar simulator (Oriel sol3A, Newport Corporation). The intensity of the light source is calibrated with a standard Si solar cell provided by Fraunhofer ISE with the mentioned mismatch factor of 1.0063. The intensity was varied using neutral density filters from 0.01 to 1 sun.
Transient Photovoltage and Photocurrent Measurements: Transient photovoltage and photocurrent of the devices were measured with an in-house-built setup. The setup comprises an LED lamp to provide steadystate white bias light, a $637~\mathsf{n m}$ wavelength laser (Vortran Stradus-637), and an Agilent 4000X oscilloscope. The LED lamp was used to get steady $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of the device. The intensity of the lamp was controlled by the external applied DC bias and was reduced with metal-mesh filters according to the necessity. The laser was controlled by the function generator of the oscillator with a frequency of ${\mathsf{10}}\ {\mathsf{H z}}$ and a pulse width of ${\mathsf{100~}}\upmu\mathsf{s}$ . The intensity of the laser was precisely controlled to keep the voltage transient amplitude under $5\%$ of the steady-state light bias. The oscilloscope records the data using 1 MΩ input impedance for the TPV measurement and $50~\Omega$ for TPC measurement. The $V_{\mathsf{O C}}$ decay curves were fitted with exponential decay to find the recombination time. The TPC curve was integrated to get the charge generated $(\Delta Q)$ in the devices due to the laser pulse. The capacitance (C) was calculated from the $C=\Delta Q/\Delta V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ relation. The total charge carrier was calculated from the integration of C versus $V_{\mathsf{O C}}$ plot. Charge carrier density $(n)$ was calculated by dividing the total charge carriers with the device volume. The density of the trap states was calculated by taking the derivative of the charge density with respect to the $V_{\mathsf{O C}}$ following a previously reported procedure.[42]
